    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `LaTeXTranslator.depart_footnote`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `depart_footnote` function is a method of the `LaTeXTranslator` class, responsible for closing a footnote in LaTeX output. It takes a single parameter, `node`, which is an instance of `Element` representing the footnote node being processed. The function checks if the translator is currently inside a parsed literal block by examining the `in_parsed_literal` attribute. If it is, the function appends the LaTeX command `\\end{footnote}` to the `body` list. If not, it appends a comment character `%` followed by a newline character `CR`, and then the `\\end{footnote}` command. This distinction ensures that footnotes inside literal blocks are formatted correctly. The function then decrements the `in_footnote` counter by one to keep track of the nesting level of footnotes. The function does not return any value; it modifies the `body` list in place and updates the `in_footnote` counter. It does not handle any errors or edge cases explicitly; it assumes that the `node` parameter is always a valid `Element` instance and that the `in_parsed_literal` and `in_footnote` attributes are properly managed elsewhere in the code. The side effects of this function include modifying the `body` list to include the closing LaTeX command for the footnote and updating the `in_footnote` counter to reflect the current nesting level of footnotes.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which funct